    "content": "---\ntitle: SVA Research Guide Redesign\nproject_type: UX Research\nrole: UX Researcher on a collaborative team\ntimeline: 4 months\ntools: Tobii Pro X2, Tobii Pro Lab, Figma, Adobe Premiere\nurl: https://sakshirane.com/work/sva\nkeywords: research, usability testing, eye tracking, academic platforms\n---\n\n🧠 Context & Challenge\n\nDuring my time working on SVA’s digital research guides, I discovered that the School of Visual Arts had a rich repository of online academic resources, but students struggled to navigate it effectively. Research guides existed, but users didn’t use them as intended — not because the content was bad, but because the experience wasn’t aligned with how users naturally browse and make decisions online.\n\nThe SVA Library was also overwhelmed with repetitive questions from students who couldn’t find what they needed on their own. This revealed a core UX problem: the tools weren’t helping students discover and access content efficiently.\n\n🎯 Problem Statement\n\nHow might we make it easier for students to find the right resources in SVA’s research guides?\nThe existing search and navigation patterns caused confusion, information overload, and inefficient search behaviors — students often resorted to guesswork rather than systematic exploration.\n\n👥 Research Approach\n\nI worked with a research team to evaluate the experience through a three-pronged research methodology:\n\nEye-Tracking for Task Completion\n\nSystem Usability Scale (SUS) Surveys\n\nHeuristic Evaluation & Usability Testing\nThis combination helped me directly observe how users look, think, and behave — not just what they say they do.\n\n🔍 Key Insights\n🔎 Insight 1 — Navigation Patterns Are Not What You Think\n\nParticipants didn’t use the site like a typical research portal. Instead of diving into paragraphs, they scanned for visual cues, headings, and familiar keywords. Dense blocks of text caused them to skip content entirely.\n\n🔎 Insight 2 — Search Was Underperforming\n\nEven though students preferred the search bar, it often failed to return useful results for realistic queries like “history of American fashion.” Many simply gave up and tried to eyeball their way through resource lists.\n\n🔎 Insight 3 — Layout Increased Cognitive Load\n\nThe three-column structure forced users to make unnecessary decisions (left vs right vs center flow), which slowed down task completion and reduced focus.\n\n🔎 Insight 4 — Visual Hierarchy Was Weak\n\nSmall labels and undifferentiated text meant users struggled to parse sections — they got lost scrolling back and forth without a clear anchor point.\n\n🧪 Interpreting the study\n\nWe combined eye-tracking observations, task completion, SUS responses, and qualitative feedback to identify navigation and search problems. This was the SVA research-guides project; the older-adult and caregiver context belongs to ERbuddy.\n\nEarlier materials gave different task-success figures without a shared denominator or measurement definition. Those percentages are omitted here until the underlying study records can establish which tasks, participants, and study phase they describe.\n\n💡 Design Recommendations\n\nBased on synthesized data from eye-tracking, quotes, click patterns, and SUS scores, our team developed recommendations in three focus areas:\n\n🎯 1. Improve Scanability\n\nStudents scan first, read second. Our recommendations included:\n\nLarger logos and icons\n\nBold, descriptive headings\n\nKeyword tags for resource previews\nThese help users spot relevant content at a glance.\n\n🎯 2. Strengthen Search Functionality\n\nA better search should:\n\nSupport complex phrases (e.g., “history of American fashion”)\n\nAuto-correct typos\n\nReturn broader semantically relevant results\nThis minimizes frustration and empowers users to trust the search tool again.\n\n🎯 3. Structure Content to Reduce Cognitive Load\n\nInstead of sprawling columns:\n\nUse tabs to filter relevant sections\n\nGroup related resources under clear headers\nThis gives users anchor points to reference rather than forcing them to scroll endlessly.\n\n📣 Client Feedback\n\n“Great recommendations from this group for reducing item and database descriptions on our guides, and for a better way to organize content on the exemplary guide, Design Archives. We can use these suggestions to apply system-wide updates to all of our guides.”\n— Digital Services Librarian, SVA Library\n\n🧠 What I Learned\n\nThis project sharpened my skills in translating raw behavioral data into clear design narratives. Seeing exactly where users looked — and where they didn’t — taught me that good UX research isn’t just about what users want, but how they actually behave.\n\nI also learned how to:\n\nCombine qualitative quotes with quantitative eye data\n\nCraft solutions that are practical and measurable\n\nCommunicate research insights in ways stakeholders can act on confidently"
