    "content": "Project Type: Visual Design, Design System, Conversational AI\nRole: Founding Product Designer\nTimeline: 4 Months\nClient: Lawspeak\nTools: Figma, Jira, Notion\nURL: https://sakshirane.com/work/lawspeak\n\n🧠 Context & Problem\n\nAs I stepped into LawSpeak, the core challenge was clear: legal professionals were spending far too many hours on repetitive, routine tasks that demanded precision — drafting, editing, translating, reviewing, and explaining legal text — and these tasks were pulling attention away from high-value strategic work like case strategy and client counsel.\n\nThe business needed a workspace that wasn’t optional — something lawyers must rely on — with accuracy, speed, and trust at the forefront.\n\n🎯 Goal & Vision\n\nI approached LawSpeak with a simple north star:\n\nBuild something that feels like an extension of a legal professional’s workflow — not an external tool to wrestle with.\n\nThis meant focusing on tool efficiency, clarity, and trust, not just flashy AI features.\n\n📌 Jobs To Be Done\n\nAfter interviewing paralegals, associates, and solo practitioners, I distilled their needs into actionable themes:\n\n✔️ Save Time — Not Just Clicks\n\nUsers didn’t want more screens or bells and whistles — they wanted every interaction to be as frictionless as possible. For example:\n\nUpload a draft → translation done\n\nUpload a contract → draft suggestions ready in minutes\nThese micro-efficiencies compounded into hours saved.\n\n✔️ Explain, Don’t Replace\n\nLegal professionals don’t want AI that “practices law.” They want AI that helps them understand legal text — neutral, accurate, and context-aware.\n\nDesigned the assistant to clarify legal language\n\nAlways cite source clauses\n\nAvoid advice/interpretation that could be construed as practicing law\n\n✔️ Speak Their Language\n\nRegional lawyers often serve clients in multiple languages. Traditional legal tools rarely support vernacular needs.\n\nBuilt an integrated English ↔ other language dual pane translation UI\n\nClear language toggles made switching instant and intuitive\n\n✔️ One Tool, Multiple Tasks\n\nLawyers wanted everything related to a case in one workspace — from file management to history tracking to AI assistance.\n\nDesigned a modular dashboard with:\n\nFile management\n\nVersion history\n\nAI drafting and explanation tools\n\nActivity tracking\n\n📊 Solution Highlights\n✍️ Drafting Documents with AI Assistance\n\nTraditionally, drafting a contract would take hours:\n\nUpload template or start from scratch\n\nGet clauses suggested instantly\n\nEdit with track-changes style UI\nResult: what used to take hours now takes ~20–30 minutes of focused iteration.\n\n🗣️ Explaining Legal Jargon\n\nClients frequently ask things like “What does this clause mean for me?”\nRather than leave lawyers to paraphrase or simplify on their own:\n\nHighlight any clause\n\nClick “Explain This”\n\nGet clear, client-friendly language + legal context\nThis saves time and improves quality of client communication.\n\n🗂 Collaborative Document Review\n\nEmail chains and Slack threads were slowing approval cycles:\n\nBuilt a Google Docs-style review interface tailored to legal workflows\n\nComments, suggestions, approvals, and history all live in context\n\nVersion control was unified, structured, and easy to navigate\n\n🌐 Translation for Regional Clients\n\nLegal documents often need accurate bilingual support:\n\nSingle click “Translate” flows\n\nDual pane views for side-by-side comparison\nThis not only sped up delivery but protected lawyers against miscommunication risks.\n\n🤔 What I Learned\n\nDesigning LawSpeak shifted how I think about AI in professional tools:\n\n🧩 Simplicity is clarity, not fewer features\n\nEarly on, I wanted to strip things down. But the real win came when I organized complexity into clear, context-aware pathways, not empty minimalism.\n\n🤝 Trust is everything in a high-stakes field\n\nLegal professionals are skeptical — with good reason.\n\nAI suggestions needed to be transparent\n\nLanguage needed to be neutral\n\nOutputs needed to be verifiable against source text\nBuilding trust meant designing with explainability as a feature, not an afterthought.\n\n💡 Focus on real needs, not what the tech can do\n\nThe turning point was when I stopped asking, “What can the AI do?” and started asking,\n\n“What does the lawyer actually need right now?”\nThat mindset shifted LawSpeak from a cool AI app to a genuinely useful legal workspace.\n\n🎯 Impact\n\nHours saved per user per week — from repetitive grunt work to strategic thinking\n\nBetter client communication — clear explanations, faster turnaround\n\nMore control, less fear — AI that assists, not overtakes\n\nEvery design decision reinforced what I’ve since carried into every project: clarity and control beat complexity, every time."
